  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 300 AM EDT Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Patchy fog possible early this morning. Partly\nto mostly sunny and a bit warmer today. A short-lived warming \ntrend continues today, with above normal temperatures returning\nfor late in the week, along with dry conditions.\n\nThe latest WX analysis indicates an upper level trough just\noffshore of the mid-Atlantic and northeast coast. To the west, \na mid-upper level ridge continues to re-develop over the  \nwestern Gulf coast across the mid-south. At the surface, weak \nhigh pressure is centered across the local area and will push\ntoward the mid-Atlantic coast by this evening. \n\nFinally getting a bit of clearing to the west, which has cleared\nthe way for some areas of fog, mainly over the Northern Neck to\njust north of RIC metro and over to parts of the VA piedmont. \nGuidance has backed off on the dense fog potential, but we \nshould see some brief VSBY restrictions for the morning rush in \nthese areas. Otherwise, any fog and stratocu should lift by \nmid-morning and we should finally see at least a partly sunny \nday across much of the region, as the low level flow shifts to \nthe S-SE by afternoon with the high sliding offshore this \nevening. Look for highs today right around 80F across the far \nnorth, low-mid 80s elsewhere.\n\nLooking ahead, there remains higher confidence that building\nheights will allow for temperatures becoming very warm Thursday\nand Friday as the low level S-SW flow increases. Still not \nanticipating record highs, but highs into the upper 80s \nThursday, with upper 80s to lower 90s Friday is about 10-15 F \nabove average for the start of October and qualifies as \n\"unseasonably warm\". \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...An unsettled weather pattern with cooler and \nwetter conditions appears likely for this weekend into early \nnext week.\n\nAn increasingly unsettled pattern looks to take shape for the\nweekend into early next week. A deep closed low currently over \nthe Desert Southwest into the Intermountain West looks to open \nup by midweek as it absorbs the mid-level remnants of TC Polo \nand lifts across the central and southern Plains. This system \nwill eventually become absorbed into a northern stream trough \ndigging across the interior Northeast into the northern Mid- \nAtlantic over the weekend. This will serve to dampen the Southeast\nridge and allow an influx of Gulf and Pacific moisture across \nthe Gulf Coast and Mid-South, likely reaching our region over \nthe weekend. One signal common to all guidance, even this far \nout, is a notable increase in PWATs, with values potentially \nreaching 1.5-2\" by Saturday night/Sunday. While there remains \nsome model differences, trends are favoring pushing that front \nsouth through much of the area on Saturday, with the front then \nslowing or stalling Sat night, as additional and likely more \nsignificant swaths of moisture rides along the front later in \nthe weekend into early next week. While some scattered showers\nare expected to accompany the initial frontal passage and wind \nshift on Saturday, the higher chances and more widespread\nrainfall has trended a bit later into Sat night/early Sunday. \nPeriods of rain are then likely to continue through\nSunday/Sunday night into Monday morning. There is still plenty \nof time to monitor the trends over the next several days, but \nlocally heavy rainfall is certainly possible (although much of \nthis would be beneficial given ongoing drought). \n\nIn terms of temperatures, increased clouds/precip and onshore \nflow favor cooler temps over the weekend into early next week, \nbut not abnormally so. Highs Saturday likely range through the \n70s and 80s, with higher PoPs and cloud cover bringing cooler \nupper 60s and 70s Sunday and Monday. Latest models indicate a \nstrong enough push of drier air associated with high pressure \nbuilding in from the north into the middle of next week, which \nwould bring below normal temperatures with highs in the 60s and \nlows into the 40s and 50s- this is still more uncertain given \nthe range.",
